  "answer_text": "Great question! Interacting with someone who has a lazy eye (also known as strabismus or amblyopia) can feel a bit awkward at first, but it doesn't need to be. Here are some helpful tips:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 1. **Look at the Eye That's Looking at You**\nLook at the eye which is looking at you. The person will notice if you are looking at the other eye, and even if you don't intend to be rude, it will feel to them as though you are staring at their \"wonky\" eye.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 2. **Remember \u2014 They ARE Making Eye Contact With You**\nWhat can throw you off is that it looks like one of their eyes is looking at you and one is looking somewhere else, which can make it feel like they're not really making eye contact. Keep in mind that both their eyes are in fact looking at you \u2014 one of them just has a hard time staying centered.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 3. **Don't Pause or Get Distracted \u2014 Just Carry On**\nFor most people with a lazy eye, they feel like they're staring right at you. So don't pause and wonder if their attention is on you \u2014 just carry on with what you're saying.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 4. **Try Softening/Blurring Your Focus**\nIf there's something unusual on someone's face that you don't want to stare at rudely, blurring your eyes lets you look straight at them and listen better. This technique can help you maintain a natural gaze without fixating on either eye too intensely.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 5. **It's Okay to Shift Your Gaze Naturally**\nRelax and give yourself permission to look at the non-dominant eye once in a while. This is natural in any conversation \u2014 we all shift focus slightly between eyes during normal eye contact.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 6. **With Close Friends, You Can Acknowledge It**\nIf you can't concentrate because of your friend's situation, acknowledge it instead of avoiding eye contact. Even if they don't look at themselves all the time, they know it's distracting. For a friend, co-worker, or family member, gently acknowledging it can lighten a possibly awkward situation.\n\nHowever, if you're talking to someone you'll probably never see again, it's not worth making them feel self-conscious about their situation. If you really feel uneasy, find another point of their face to focus on and wrap up the conversation naturally.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc41\ufe0f 7. **Give It Time**\nMuch like anything unusual about a person, you will stop noticing it as you get to know them and other things about that person start to form your dominant image.\n\nThe most important thing is to be respectful, relaxed, and natural \u2014 the person with the lazy eye is very aware of the situation, and your calm, focused attitude will make the interaction comfortable for both of you.",
